{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01PdPkEt9qw9JseF5qwNgfmj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t/*\n2\t * gapbuffer.h\n3\t * Defines the interface for working with the gapbuffer data structure.\n4\t * Note that the gapbuffer datatype should not be manually populated.\n5\t * Use the interface methods for creating/destroying the structure.\n6\t *\n7\t * */\n8\t\n9\t#ifndef TED_GAP_H\n10\t#define TED_GAP_H\n11\t\n12\t#define MEM_ERROR 128\n13\t\n14\t/*\n15\t * Gap Buffer Data structure\n16\t * A buffer that uses a \"gap\" within a string to allow addition of new characters to it.\n17\t * The length of the buffer is the length of the gap + the length of the string\n18\t *\n19\t * Gap Buffer implements the buffer interface defined in base.h\n20\t *\n21\t * Implementation:\n22\t * - The string is seen as a separate object starting from buffer[0] and extending str_len characters.\n23\t * - The gap is always somewhere between this string, or at the prefix or suffix.\n24\t * - The length of the buffer is always the sum of the string length and the gap length.\n25\t * - The string is buffer[0:gap_loc-1] + buffer[(gap_loc+gap_len):(gap_loc+gap_len) + (str_len-gap_loc)]:\n26\t *      - 0 to gap_loc-1 is the string up to the gap\n27\t *      - (gap_loc+gap_len) is the index right after the gap\n28\t *      - (gap_loc+gap_len) + (str_len-gap_loc-1) is the last index of the string.\n29\t * */\n30\t\n31\ttypedef struct GapBuffer {\n32\t    char* buffer;  // Buffer containing string and gap\n33\t    int str_len;    // Length of the string\n34\t    int gap_len;    // Length of the gap\n35\t    int gap_loc;    // Gap location as an offset from the start of the buffer\n36\t} GapBuffer;\n37\t\n38\t\n39\t\n40\t/*\n41\t * INTERFACES\n42\t * */\n43\t\n44\t/*\n45\t * Creates and initializes a new gap buffer. Initially the gap is of size `capacity`\n46\t * returns NULL on fail.\n47\t * */\n48\tGapBuffer* CreateGapBuffer(int capacity);\n49\t\n50\t\n51\t/*\n52\t * DestroyGapBuffer Safely deallocates a gapbuffer. The pointer given is set to NULL after.\n53\t * Passing a NULL pointer does nothing.\n54\t * buffer: pointer to an allocated gapbuffer.\n55\t * */\n56\tvoid DestroyGapBuffer(GapBuffer* instance);\n57\t\n58\t\n59\t/*\n60\t * Inserts a character into the gap buffer. Resizes the buffer and the gap if necessary.\n61\t * Returns 0 if successful or MEM_ERROR\n62\t * */\n63\tint GapBufferInsertChar(GapBuffer* instance, char ch);\n64\t\n65\t\n66\t/*\n67\t * Backspace deletes a character from the prefix of the gap, extending the gap length.\n68\t * */\n69\tvoid GapBufferBackSpace(GapBuffer* instance);\n70\t\n71\t\n72\t/*\n73\t * Moves the gap to a new location in the string. If the location is greater than str_len,\n74\t * it moves to the end of the string. if less than 0, moves to the start of the string\n75\t * */\n76\tint GapBufferMoveGap(GapBuffer* instance, int location);\n77\t\n78\t\n79\t/*\n80\t * GetString returns a pointer to an allocated copy of the current string in the gapbuffer.\n81\t * */\n82\tchar* GapBufferGetString(GapBuffer* instance);\n83\t\n84\t\n85\t/*\n86\t * Splits the buffer at the cursor location, returning a new buffer of the same capacity as the original\n87\t * with the second half of the string. The original buffer will contain the first half of the string,\n88\t * with the gap extending to fill the capacity.\n89\t *\n90\t * return a pointer to the new buffer, or NULL on failure.\n91\t * */\n92\tGapBuffer* GapBufferSplit(GapBuffer* instance);\n93\t\n94\t\n95\t/*\n96\t * Create a gap buffer containing the contents of str. The gap length is initialized to 'gap_len'.\n97\t * The gap is always positioned at the end of the string.\n98\t * if str is invalid, defaults to CreateGapBuffer(gap_len);\n99\t *\n100\t * returns an initialized GapBuffer* or NULL on error\n101\t * */\n102\tGapBuffer* CreateGapBufferFromString(char* str, int gap_len);\n103\t\n104\t\n105\t/*\n106\t * Given an index i, return the character at the location i.\n107\t * The gap is ignored; acts similar to string index.\n108\t * domain for i = [0, strlen-1]\n109\t * returns null byte if the instance is invalid, the string is empty, or i is out of domain\n110\t * */\n111\tchar GapBufferCharAt(GapBuffer* instance, int i);\n112\t\n113\t\n114\t#endif //TED_GAP_H\n115\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0bb83036-4037-40a2-905a-2aca87fa710f","uuid":"10b4a4f4-723a-4e43-ab26-335f7cae9274","timestamp":"2026-06-06T02:08:22.588Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-SLaew2/project/c_src/buffer/gap.h","content":"/*\n * gapbuffer.h\n * Defines the interface for working with the gapbuffer data structure.\n * Note that the gapbuffer datatype should not be manually populated.\n * Use the interface methods for creating/destroying the structure.\n *\n * */\n\n#ifndef TED_GAP_H\n#define TED_GAP_H\n\n#define MEM_ERROR 128\n\n/*\n * Gap Buffer Data structure\n * A buffer that uses a \"gap\" within a string to allow addition of new characters to it.\n * The length of the buffer is the length of the gap + the length of the string\n *\n * Gap Buffer implements the buffer interface defined in base.h\n *\n * Implementation:\n * - The string is seen as a separate object starting from buffer[0] and extending str_len characters.\n * - The gap is always somewhere between this string, or at the prefix or suffix.\n * - The length of the buffer is always the sum of the string length and the gap length.\n * - The string is buffer[0:gap_loc-1] + buffer[(gap_loc+gap_len):(gap_loc+gap_len) + (str_len-gap_loc)]:\n *      - 0 to gap_loc-1 is the string up to the gap\n *      - (gap_loc+gap_len) is the index right after the gap\n *      - (gap_loc+gap_len) + (str_len-gap_loc-1) is the last index of the string.\n * */\n\ntypedef struct GapBuffer {\n    char* buffer;  // Buffer containing string and gap\n    int str_len;    // Length of the string\n    int gap_len;    // Length of the gap\n    int gap_loc;    // Gap location as an offset from the start of the buffer\n} GapBuffer;\n\n\n\n/*\n * INTERFACES\n * */\n\n/*\n * Creates and initializes a new gap buffer. Initially the gap is of size `capacity`\n * returns NULL on fail.\n * */\nGapBuffer* CreateGapBuffer(int capacity);\n\n\n/*\n * DestroyGapBuffer Safely deallocates a gapbuffer. The pointer given is set to NULL after.\n * Passing a NULL pointer does nothing.\n * buffer: pointer to an allocated gapbuffer.\n * */\nvoid DestroyGapBuffer(GapBuffer* instance);\n\n\n/*\n * Inserts a character into the gap buffer. Resizes the buffer and the gap if necessary.\n * Returns 0 if successful or MEM_ERROR\n * */\nint GapBufferInsertChar(GapBuffer* instance, char ch);\n\n\n/*\n * Backspace deletes a character from the prefix of the gap, extending the gap length.\n * */\nvoid GapBufferBackSpace(GapBuffer* instance);\n\n\n/*\n * Moves the gap to a new location in the string. If the location is greater than str_len,\n * it moves to the end of the string. if less than 0, moves to the start of the string\n * */\nint GapBufferMoveGap(GapBuffer* instance, int location);\n\n\n/*\n * GetString returns a pointer to an allocated copy of the current string in the gapbuffer.\n * */\nchar* GapBufferGetString(GapBuffer* instance);\n\n\n/*\n * Splits the buffer at the cursor location, returning a new buffer of the same capacity as the original\n * with the second half of the string. The original buffer will contain the first half of the string,\n * with the gap extending to fill the capacity.\n *\n * return a pointer to the new buffer, or NULL on failure.\n * */\nGapBuffer* GapBufferSplit(GapBuffer* instance);\n\n\n/*\n * Create a gap buffer containing the contents of str. The gap length is initialized to 'gap_len'.\n * The gap is always positioned at the end of the string.\n * if str is invalid, defaults to CreateGapBuffer(gap_len);\n *\n * returns an initialized GapBuffer* or NULL on error\n * */\nGapBuffer* CreateGapBufferFromString(char* str, int gap_len);\n\n\n/*\n * Given an index i, return the character at the location i.\n * The gap is ignored; acts similar to string index.\n * domain for i = [0, strlen-1]\n * returns null byte if the instance is invalid, the string is empty, or i is out of domain\n * */\nchar GapBufferCharAt(GapBuffer* instance, int i);\n\n\n#endif //TED_GAP_H\n","numLines":115,"startLine":1,"totalLines":115}}}
